 Sortable data table with column headers, row selection, pagination,
 and striped rows. Columns define width, alignment, and sortability.
 The widget builder composes header buttons and data label rows from
 base widget kinds.

Section: Column

  TableColumn = record {
    tc-id : Text,
    tc-label : Text,
    tc-width : Integer,
    tc-sortable : Boolean,
    tc-align : Integer
  }

  table-column : Text, Text, Integer -> TableColumn
  table-column (id) (label) (width) = TableColumn {
    tc-id = id,
    tc-label = label,
    tc-width = width,
    tc-sortable = True,
    tc-align = 0
  }

  table-column-fixed : Text, Text, Integer -> TableColumn
  table-column-fixed (id) (label) (width) = TableColumn {
    tc-id = id,
    tc-label = label,
    tc-width = width,
    tc-sortable = False,
    tc-align = 0
  }

Section: Row

  TableRow = record {
    tr-id : Text,
    tr-cells : List Text,
    tr-cell-count : Integer,
    tr-selected : Boolean
  }

  table-row : Text, List Text -> TableRow
  table-row (id) (cells) = TableRow {
    tr-id = id,
    tr-cells = cells,
    tr-cell-count = list-length cells,
    tr-selected = False
  }

Section: State

  DataTableState = record {
    dt-id : Text,
    dt-columns : List TableColumn,
    dt-col-count : Integer,
    dt-rows : List TableRow,
    dt-row-count : Integer,
    dt-sort-col : Integer,
    dt-sort-asc : Boolean,
    dt-selected-row : Integer,
    dt-multi-select : Boolean,
    dt-page-size : Integer,
    dt-page : Integer,
    dt-stripe : Boolean
  }

  data-table-new : Text, List TableColumn, List TableRow -> DataTableState
  data-table-new (id) (cols) (rows) = DataTableState {
    dt-id = id,
    dt-columns = cols,
    dt-col-count = list-length cols,
    dt-rows = rows,
    dt-row-count = list-length rows,
    dt-sort-col = -1,
    dt-sort-asc = True,
    dt-selected-row = -1,
    dt-multi-select = False,
    dt-page-size = 20,
    dt-page = 0,
    dt-stripe = True
  }

Section: Actions

  data-table-sort : DataTableState, Integer -> DataTableState
  data-table-sort (dt) (col-idx) =
    if col-idx < 0 then dt
    else if col-idx >= dt.dt-col-count then dt
    else let col = list-at (dt.dt-columns) col-idx
    in if col.tc-sortable then
      let asc = if dt.dt-sort-col == col-idx then if dt.dt-sort-asc then False else True else True
      in let sorted = dt-sort-rows (dt.dt-rows) col-idx asc (dt.dt-row-count)
      in __record-set (__record-set (__record-set dt "dt-rows" sorted) "dt-sort-col" col-idx) "dt-sort-asc" asc
    else dt

 The sort writes through list-set-at, which stores into the slot and
 hands back the same list, so it is copied first. Without the copy the
 state handed in is sorted too, and a caller holding the unsorted table
 finds it reordered underneath: this function's own type says it returns
 a new state, and the single-select path already copies for the same
 reason.

 A collation key is extracted once per row and the sort compares those,
 which is what the key design is for: the rank tables are consulted once
 per character per row rather than twice per comparison, and the
 comparison stays the `text-compare` this file already used.

  dt-sort-rows : List TableRow, Integer, Boolean, Integer -> List TableRow
  dt-sort-rows (rows) (col) (asc) (n) =
    if n <= 1 then rows
    else let copied = dt-copy-rows rows 0 n []
    in let keys = dt-key-rows copied col 0 n []
    in dt-isort copied keys asc 1 n

  dt-copy-rows : List TableRow, Integer, Integer, List TableRow -> List TableRow
  dt-copy-rows (rows) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else dt-copy-rows rows (i + 1) n (list-push acc (list-at rows i))

  dt-key-rows : List TableRow, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  dt-key-rows (rows) (col) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let k = collate-key (dt-cell-at (list-at rows i) col)
    in dt-key-rows rows col (i + 1) n (list-push acc k)

  dt-isort : List TableRow, List Text, Boolean, Integer, Integer -> List TableRow
  dt-isort (rows) (keys) (asc) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then rows
    else dt-isort (dt-insert rows keys asc i) keys asc (i + 1) n

 Two cells are ordered with text-compare and never with the ordering
 operators. Measured 2026-07-27 against the depot seed: the ordering
 operators on Text do not read the Text at all. They compare the operands
 as they were handed over rather than by content, so two freshly built
 literals answer by the order they were built in and give the same answer
 whatever the letters are: "a" is reported less than "b", "b" less than
 "a", and "a" less than "a", all True. text-compare is the primitive that
 reads the characters, and it is what SkipListText and RankedTextSet
 already order by.

 **text-compare orders by CCE code point, and that is not the alphabet.**
 CCE numbers the letters by English frequency, so e is 13, t is 14, a is
 15, d is 22, c is 24, b is 32 and z is 38, and a column sorted through it
 comes out in an order a human reads as arbitrary. That is the right order
 for an ordered container and the wrong one for a column somebody reads.

 So the cells are not compared directly at all. `Foreword chapter Collate`
 turns each into a sort key whose ENCODING order is collation order, the
 keys are extracted once per row, and `text-compare` over two keys is then
 the alphabetical comparison this column wants.

 The two lists move together. `list-set-at` writes the slot and hands
 back the same list, so swapping the keys is what keeps a row and its key
 at the same index; separating them by one swap would sort the rows by
 somebody else's key and the answer would look almost right.

  dt-insert : List TableRow, List Text, Boolean, Integer -> List TableRow
  dt-insert (rows) (keys) (asc) (i) =
    if i <= 0 then rows
    else let cur = list-at rows i
    in let prev = list-at rows (i - 1)
    in let kcur = list-at keys i
    in let kprev = list-at keys (i - 1)
    in let ord = text-compare kcur kprev
    in let swap = if asc then ord < 0 else ord > 0
    in if swap then dt-insert (dt-swap rows keys i cur prev kcur kprev) keys asc (i - 1)
    else rows

  dt-swap : List TableRow, List Text, Integer, TableRow, TableRow, Text, Text -> List TableRow
  dt-swap (rows) (keys) (i) (cur) (prev) (kcur) (kprev) =
    let moved = list-set-at (list-set-at keys i kprev) (i - 1) kcur
    in list-set-at (list-set-at rows i prev) (i - 1) cur

  dt-cell-at : TableRow, Integer -> Text
  dt-cell-at (row) (col) =
    if col < 0 then ""
    else if col >= row.tr-cell-count then ""
    else list-at (row.tr-cells) col

  data-table-select-row : DataTableState, Integer -> DataTableState
  data-table-select-row (dt) (idx) =
    if idx < 0 then dt
    else if idx >= dt.dt-row-count then dt
    else let cleared = if dt.dt-multi-select then dt.dt-rows else dt-clear-rows (dt.dt-rows) (dt.dt-row-count)
    in let row = list-at cleared idx
    in let toggled = __record-set row "tr-selected" (if row.tr-selected then False else True)
    in __record-set (__record-set dt "dt-rows" (list-set-at cleared idx toggled)) "dt-selected-row" idx

  dt-clear-rows : List TableRow, Integer -> List TableRow
  dt-clear-rows (rows) (n) = dt-clear-loop rows 0 n []

  dt-clear-loop : List TableRow, Integer, Integer, List TableRow -> List TableRow
  dt-clear-loop (rows) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else dt-clear-loop rows (i + 1) n (list-push acc (__record-set (list-at rows i) "tr-selected" False))

  data-table-select-all : DataTableState -> DataTableState
  data-table-select-all (dt) =
    __record-set dt "dt-rows" (dt-set-all-selected (dt.dt-rows) (dt.dt-row-count) True)

  data-table-deselect-all : DataTableState -> DataTableState
  data-table-deselect-all (dt) =
    __record-set dt "dt-rows" (dt-set-all-selected (dt.dt-rows) (dt.dt-row-count) False)

  dt-set-all-selected : List TableRow, Integer, Boolean -> List TableRow
  dt-set-all-selected (rows) (n) (val) = dt-set-sel-loop rows 0 n val []

  dt-set-sel-loop : List TableRow, Integer, Integer, Boolean, List TableRow -> List TableRow
  dt-set-sel-loop (rows) (i) (n) (val) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else dt-set-sel-loop rows (i + 1) n val (list-push acc (__record-set (list-at rows i) "tr-selected" val))

Section: Pagination

 A table with no rows still has the page the reader is looking at, so the
 count is one rather than zero. Left at zero the footer reads "Page 1 of
 0", and the page a caller is on is outside the range its own count
 declares.

  data-table-page-count : DataTableState -> Integer
  data-table-page-count (dt) =
    if dt.dt-page-size <= 0 then 1
    else if dt.dt-row-count <= 0 then 1
    else let full = dt.dt-row-count / dt.dt-page-size
    in if full * dt.dt-page-size < dt.dt-row-count then full + 1 else full

  data-table-page-next : DataTableState -> DataTableState
  data-table-page-next (dt) =
    let max-page = data-table-page-count dt - 1
    in let next = dt.dt-page + 1
    in if next > max-page then dt
    else __record-set dt "dt-page" next

  data-table-page-prev : DataTableState -> DataTableState
  data-table-page-prev (dt) =
    if dt.dt-page <= 0 then dt
    else __record-set dt "dt-page" (dt.dt-page - 1)

  data-table-visible-rows : DataTableState -> List TableRow
  data-table-visible-rows (dt) =
    let start = dt.dt-page * dt.dt-page-size
    in let end-idx = start + dt.dt-page-size
    in let actual-end = if end-idx > dt.dt-row-count then dt.dt-row-count else end-idx
    in dt-slice-rows (dt.dt-rows) start actual-end []

  dt-slice-rows : List TableRow, Integer, Integer, List TableRow -> List TableRow
  dt-slice-rows (rows) (i) (end-idx) (acc) =
    if i >= end-idx then acc
    else dt-slice-rows rows (i + 1) end-idx (list-push acc (list-at rows i))

Section: Widget Building

  data-table-widget : DataTableState -> WidgetNode
  data-table-widget (dt) =
    let header = dt-header-widget dt
    in let vis = data-table-visible-rows dt
    in let body-rows = dt-body-widgets dt vis 0 (list-length vis) (dt.dt-page * dt.dt-page-size) []
    in let footer = dt-footer-widget dt
    in widget-panel (dt.dt-id) DirColumn 0 ([header] & body-rows & [footer])

  dt-header-widget : DataTableState -> WidgetNode
  dt-header-widget (dt) =
    let cols = dt-header-cols dt 0 (dt.dt-col-count) []
    in widget-panel (dt.dt-id & "-hdr") DirRow 1 cols

  dt-header-cols : DataTableState, Integer, Integer, List WidgetNode -> List WidgetNode
  dt-header-cols (dt) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let col = list-at (dt.dt-columns) i
    in let indicator = if dt.dt-sort-col == i then if dt.dt-sort-asc then " ^" else " v" else ""
    in let btn = widget-button (dt.dt-id & "-col-" & show i) (col.tc-label & indicator)
    in let sized = widget-set-min btn (col.tc-width) 24
    in dt-header-cols dt (i + 1) n (list-push acc sized)

  dt-body-widgets : DataTableState, List TableRow, Integer, Integer, Integer, List WidgetNode -> List WidgetNode
  dt-body-widgets (dt) (rows) (i) (n) (base-idx) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let row = list-at rows i
    in let row-widget = dt-row-widget dt row (base-idx + i) i
    in dt-body-widgets dt rows (i + 1) n base-idx (list-push acc row-widget)

 A stripe is decoration and a selection is state, so the selection wins.
 The stripe used to be applied last and unconditionally, which set the
 hover state over the pressed state a selected row had just been given:
 selecting an odd row looked exactly like selecting nothing, on every
 other row, while the row's own tr-selected said otherwise.

  dt-row-widget : DataTableState, TableRow, Integer, Integer -> WidgetNode
  dt-row-widget (dt) (row) (abs-idx) (vis-idx) =
    let cells = dt-cell-widgets dt row 0 (dt.dt-col-count) []
    in let row-w = widget-panel (dt.dt-id & "-row-" & show abs-idx) DirRow 1 cells
    in if row.tr-selected then widget-set-state row-w state-pressed
    else if dt.dt-stripe then
      let even = vis-idx - (vis-idx / 2) * 2
      in if even == 0 then row-w else widget-set-state row-w state-hover
    else row-w

  dt-cell-widgets : DataTableState, TableRow, Integer, Integer, List WidgetNode -> List WidgetNode
  dt-cell-widgets (dt) (row) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let col = list-at (dt.dt-columns) i
    in let txt = dt-cell-at row i
    in let lbl = widget-label (dt.dt-id & "-cell-" & row.tr-id & "-" & show i) txt
    in let sized = widget-set-min lbl (col.tc-width) 16
    in dt-cell-widgets dt row (i + 1) n (list-push acc sized)

  dt-footer-widget : DataTableState -> WidgetNode
  dt-footer-widget (dt) =
    let page-count = data-table-page-count dt
    in let info = widget-label (dt.dt-id & "-info") ("Page " & show (dt.dt-page + 1) & " of " & show page-count & " | " & show (dt.dt-row-count) & " rows")
    in let prev-btn = widget-button (dt.dt-id & "-prev") "< Prev"
    in let next-btn = widget-button (dt.dt-id & "-next") "Next >"
    in widget-panel (dt.dt-id & "-footer") DirRow 8 [prev-btn, info, next-btn]

Section: Keyboard Handling

 A key is what `UI chapter KeyInput` says it is: a character is its CCE
 code point and a named key is a constant at or above `key-named-base`.
 These arms tested 38, 40, 13, 33 and 34, which are Windows virtual key
 codes and, read as CCE, ordinary letters. Under the contract the quire
 publishes every one of them missed, so a table could not be walked,
 selected from or paged at all: measured against seed 8B7A02EC on a
 twenty-five row two-page table, every key left `dt-selected-row` at -1
 and `dt-page` at 0.

 Enter selects whatever row the navigation has reached, so on a table
 nobody has moved in yet it correctly does nothing: `dt-selected-row`
 starts at -1 and `data-table-select-row` refuses a negative index.

  data-table-handle-key : DataTableState, Integer -> DataTableState
  data-table-handle-key (dt) (key) =
    if key == key-up then dt-nav-up dt
    else if key == key-down then dt-nav-down dt
    else if key == key-enter then data-table-select-row dt (dt.dt-selected-row)
    else if key == key-pgup then data-table-page-prev dt
    else if key == key-pgdn then data-table-page-next dt
    else dt

  dt-nav-up : DataTableState -> DataTableState
  dt-nav-up (dt) =
    let prev = dt.dt-selected-row - 1
    in if prev < 0 then dt
    else __record-set dt "dt-selected-row" prev

  dt-nav-down : DataTableState -> DataTableState
  dt-nav-down (dt) =
    let next = dt.dt-selected-row + 1
    in if next >= dt.dt-row-count then dt
    else __record-set dt "dt-selected-row" next

Section: Formatting

  format-data-table : DataTableState -> Text
  format-data-table (dt) =
    dt.dt-id & " cols=" & show (dt.dt-col-count) & " rows=" & show (dt.dt-row-count)
    & " page=" & show (dt.dt-page + 1) & "/" & show (data-table-page-count dt)

Page 1
